  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMid level ridge centered over the southeast expands north into the\nregion. Height and thickness rises will result in warmer conditions\nwith afternoon high temperatures peaking out from the mid 80s\nnortheast to the lower 90s southwest. An increase in surface dew \npoints will accompany the warmer temperatures with readings in the \nmid 60s to 70. The combination of heat and humidity will result in \napparent temperatures topping out from near 90 northeast to near 100 \nsouthwest. \n\nMain storm activity to remain north and northwest of ILN/s area\naround the periphery of the mid level ridge during the daylight\nhours. A frontal boundary encroaching into the Great Lakes will \nlead to increasing precipitation chances overnight into Wednesday \nmorning. Storms that are expected to develop to our northwest this\nevening are expected to move into west central Ohio late this\nevening through the overnight hours. SPC Day 1 outlook has adjusted \nthe marginal risk area a little farther north. This looks very\nreasonable with some of the CAM solutions showing scattered convection\nmoving into ILN/s northern counties. The storms are expected to be\nweakening and shear and instability is not overly impressive, but \ncan not rule out a marginal severe threat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nHeat and humidity will be observed through Thursday with ILN/s area\non the northern periphery of the mid level ridge. Convective chances\nlook most favorable across our northern counties - from the frontal \nzone on the northern edge of the warm sector. Although convection \nand clouds could alter the temperature forecast, at this time \nWednesday and Thursday look to be the warmest days. With surface\ndewpoints getting into the lower 70s, some areas in the southern \nhalf of the forecast area may approach heat advisory criteria (100 \ndegree heat indices) on both days. \n\nLooking at forecast soundings - the environment will be characterized \nby marginal shear and moderate instability which suggests a lack of \nan organized severe weather setup but a strong to severe storm can \nnot be ruled out. Heavy rain looks to be a threat with PWAT values \naround 2 inches and the potential for several rounds of storms. \n\nThe ridge begins to break down on Friday and into the weekend, \noffering slightly cooler temperatures. Thunderstorm chances continue\nin the west- northwest flow pattern around the ridge centered over \nthe Lower MS Valley.",
